<HTML>
<Title>Directory and File Structure</Title>
<BODY>
<h1>Directory and File Structure</h1>
<p>
After initial start-up of Emma 02 a default directory and file structure will be made available according to the subsections below. These files can be re-installed at any time with the menu option below: 
</p>
<p>
<IMG SRC="images/menu_reinstall.png" border="0" width="420" height ="106">
</p>
<p>
The location for the data directory is default set to:
</p>
<UL>
<LI>Windows Vista and later: C:\\Users\\<I>username</I>\\AppData\\Roaming\\Emma 02
<LI>XP: C:\\Documents and Settings\\<I>username</I>\\Application Data\\Emma 02
<LI>Mac OSX: ~/Emma 02
<LI>Linux: ~/emma_02_data
</UL>
<p>
Note that when an older versions of Emma 02 was used previously the default data directory might be set to:
</p>
<UL>
<LI>Mac OSX: ~/Documents/emma_02
<LI>Linux: ~/Documents/emma_02
</UL>
</p>
<p>
To change the location of these files use the menu item below:
</p>
<p>
<IMG SRC="images/menu_change_data_dir.png" border="0" width="324" height ="106">
</p>
<p>
A small window will open where with the 'browse' button (highlighted in yellow) a new location can be selected. After this 2 options are available: either to only save the new location ('Save' button, highlighted in orange) or save and move the existing data directory and files to the new location ('Move & Save' button, highlighted in blue). If the save only option is used the data folder should be moved manually. When moving the folder to another disk the process could take a few minutes (depending on the disk speed). In all cases all settings will be set to default as in the ''Set Default (All)' menu action.
</p>
<p>
<IMG SRC="images/menu_change_data_dir_window.png" border="0" width="381" height ="184">
</p>
<p>
<B>Note:</B> When installing a new version of Emma 02 the data directory will NOT be re-installed automatically to avoid overwriting changes made by users. To install all the latest files from the Emma 02 installation package follow the instructions at the top of this page. 
</p>
<h2>CDP18S020</h2>
<p>
Directory including the <a href="https://www.emma02.hobby-site.com/cdp18s020.html">CDP18S020 Evaluation Kit</a> UT4 ROM and Tiny Basic.
</p>
<h2>Chip 8</h2>
<p>
Directory including Chip 8, Chip 8X, Chip 10 and Super-chip software. This software can be used in the Cosmac VIP, TMC 2000, Nano, Studio II, Studio III / Victory MPT-02 and where indicated in the ETI660 and Studio IV. Sub directories include: 
</p>
<UL>
<li>Chip-8 Demos
<li>Chip-8 ETI660 Hybrids, SW only running on ETI660 (not compatible with other chip 8 interpreters)
<li>Chip-8 Games
<li>Chip-8 Hires, SW using an adapted chip-8 interpreter allowing for a higher resolution
<li>Chip-8 Hybrids, SW combining 1802 and Chip 8
<li>Chip-8 Programs
<li>Chip-8X, SW using the chip-8X interpreter
<li>Chip-10, SW using the chip-10 interpreter and HW
<li>Super-chip Games, SW using my own <a href="https://www.emma02.hobby-site.com/pseudo_super_chip.html">Super-chip</a> interpreter for a modified VIP (chip10 modification) and Studio IV 
</UL>
<h2>Cidelsa</h2>
<p>
All available <a href="https://www.emma02.hobby-site.com/cidelsa.html">Cidelsa</a> ROMs and CrossLib games: Xbomber, Xchase, Xshoot.
</p>
<h2>CoinArcade</h2>
<p>
All available <a href="https://www.emma02.hobby-site.com/coin_arcade.html">RCA Video Coin Arcade</a> ROMs.
</p>
<h2>Comx</h2>
<p>
Directory including all available <a href="https://www.emma02.hobby-site.com/comx.html">COMX-35</a> system and expansion ROM files. With the sub directories:
</p>
<h3>Comx/Demo</h3>
<p>
Some F&M demo's that never made it to full games.
</p>
<h3>Comx/Disks</h3>
<p>
Disk images with COMX DOS 1.4, F&M Disk Tools, selection of miscellaneous software and some specific software that can only be used from disk (like 'Hit It' and 'Comxstar').
</p>
<h3>Comx/Educatief</h3>
<p>
All available Dutch educational programs. 
</p>
<h3>Comx/Educational</h3>
<p>
All available English educational programs. 
</p>
<h3>Comx/F&M Basic</h3>
<p>
Example software written for F&M Basic
</p>
<h3>Comx/F&M EPROM Switchboard</h3>
<p>
F&M EPROM Switchboard firmware files.
</p>
<h3>Comx/Games</h3>
<p>
All available English games.
</p>
<h3>Comx/Hulpmiddelen - Utilities</h3>
<p>
All available utilities. 
</p>
<h3>Comx/Plotter</h3>
<p>
Example software written for the COMX PL-80 plotter. Note that some plotter software can only be used from disk and is available in disk images in the Disks directory.
</p>
<h3>Comx/Programmas</h3>
<p>
All available Dutch programs.
</p>
<h3>Comx/Programs</h3>
<p>
All available English programs.
</p>
<h3>Comx/Spellen</h3>
<p>
All available Dutch games.
</p>
<h3>Comx/Talen - Languages</h3>
<p>
All available computer languages.
</p>
<h3>Comx/Usb FW</h3>
<p>
COMX Super Board firmware files, for more info please see our <a href="http://www.comx35.com/superboard.html">COMX Super Board</a> page.
</p>
<h2>Conic</h2>
<p>
Directory including the <a href="https://www.emma02.hobby-site.com/victory.html">RCA Studio III / Conic</a> ROM files, multi-cart ROM, Studio II chip 8 and some cartridge files (with st2 extension) for some additional game ROMs.
</p>
<h2>CosmacGameSystem</h2>
<p>
All available <a href="https://www.emma02.hobby-site.com/rca_game_system.html">RCA COSMAC Computer Game System</a> ROMs.
</p>
<h2>Cosmicos</h2>
<p>
Directory including some <a href="https://www.emma02.hobby-site.com/cosmicos.html">Cosmicos</a> software including the Ascii monitor, hex and UT4 monitor, hex monitor, hexmops and mops.
</p>
<h2>Cybervision</h2>
<p>
All available <a href="https://www.emma02.hobby-site.com/cybervision.html">Cybervision 2001</a> firmware.
</p>
<h2>Elf</h2>
<p>
Directory including some Elf software including ElfOs, Giant Board Monitor, Fig Forth and Quest Super Basic (for copyright details see <a href="https://www.emma02.hobby-site.com/basic_quest_6_0.html">Quest Super Basic</a>)
</p>
<h3>Bisoio</h3>
<p>
BIOSIO files for use with Elf/Os 0.2.8.
</p>
<h3>Bisoio</h3>
<p>
BIOSIO PS2 files for use with Elf/Os 0.2.8.
</p>
<h3>Elf/elf-emulation.com</h3>
<p>
Directory including Elf software available on the <a href="http://www.elf-emulation.com">Elf/1802 Emulation</a> site. Most software is included with source and help files, stored in sub directories. All software in this directory is copyright by Michael H Riley
</p>
<p>
To run the following software make sure to select bios1.0.4.rom as main ROM and use the VT100 terminal emulation. Start or auto boot hex address as follows:<br>
'RcForth v1.0.hex': 0<br>
'RcForth Rom Version.hex': E000<br>
'RcLisp Rom Version.hex': D000<br>
'EdtAsm': c000<br>
'EdtAsm Rom Version': 8000<br>
'Bascom Tiny Basic Compiler': 8600<br>
'RcBAsic L1': 8000<br>
'RcBacis L2': 8000<br>
</p>
<p>
'Dice Rolling.hex' and 'Extended ETOPS' both start at hex 0 and do not need the bios. Note that 
dice rolling only works on the Cosmac Elf version.
</p>
<h3>Fig Forth 1802</h3>
<p>
Fig Forth assembler, list and hex file.
</p>
<h3>Elf/Games</h3>
<p>
Directory with some Elf Games. Please see the configurations part of section <a href="https://www.emma02.hobby-site.com/elf.html">Cosmac Elf</a> for instructions of loading the Game Mode configuration.
</p>
<h3>Elf/Music</h3>
<p>
Directory with some Elf Music software and Christmas songs. Please see the configurations part of section <a href="https://www.emma02.hobby-site.com/elf.html">Cosmac Elf</a> for instructions of loading the Music Machine configuration.
</p>
<h3>Elf/Netronics Tiny Basic</h3>
<p>
Directory with Netronics Tiny Basic assembler code and manual.
</p>
<h3>Elf/Pico Elf</h3>
<p>
Directory with all <a href="https://www.emma02.hobby-site.com/picoelf.html">Pico/Elf V2</a> system ROMs as well as some command and lbr files.
</p>
<h3>Elf/Pico Elf/TMS9918</h3>
<p>
Directory with multiple TMS9918 examples written by Glenn Jolly. All examples should be loaded and started at hex address 0x2000.
</p>
<h3>Elf/QuestData</h3>
<p>
Directory with some example code from Quest Data.
</p>
<h3>Elf/SuperBasic</h3>
<p>
Directory with some <a href="https://www.emma02.hobby-site.com/basic_quest_6_0.html">Quest Super Basic</a> example programs.
</p>
<h3>Elf/TinyBasic</h3>
<p>
Directory with some Tiny Basic example programs.
</p>
<h3>Elf/TinyosTS</h3>
<p>
Netronics TinyBasic for the Elf/Os operating system.
</p>
<h3>Elf/VDU</h3>
<p>
Software supporting the ACE VDU board. Two configurations are provided 'VDU / Ascii keyboard' and 'VDU / Hex keypad', the first for Tiny basic and Monitor, the second for the games. Please see the configurations part of section <a href="https://www.emma02.hobby-site.com/elf.html">Cosmac Elf</a> for instructions of loading these configuration.
</p>
